WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:07.000
In 1985, it only took four words to rocket
Dolph Lundgren to international stardom.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.660
"I must break you."

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:17.700
As Soviet punching machine Ivan Drago, the
towering antagonist of Rocky IV, Lundgren

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.340
made a serious impression.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.900
In the years since, it's been revealed that
he only got into movies on a lark, as his

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:29.109
Masters' degree in chemical engineering probably
would have opened up employment options for

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:30.620
him in a different field.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:35.660
Fortunately, Hollywood has been pretty good
to the kickboxing chemist, and if you've ever

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.510
wondered whatever happened to Dolph, you may
be surprised to know that he never really

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:46.100
went away, and that, thanks to an old friend,
his profile may soon be higher than ever.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.220
Direct-to-video

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.720
After his Rocky IV success, Lundgren found
himself with no shortage of offers.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.190
He starred in 1987's notoriously ramshackle
Masters of the Universe as He-Man, and was

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:05.040
the first big-screen incarnation of Marvel's
Punisher in 1989.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.750
But after '95, the big screen offers seemed
to dry up, and Lundgren spent the next decade

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.560
and a half working steadily in the types of
films you only used to see on the bargain

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.820
shelf at Blockbuster.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.460
Writer and director

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:30.920
Lundgren's period in direct-to-video purgatory
was not without its professional rewards.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.240
As it happens, he ended up behind the camera
purely by chance.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:41.400
He explained to Grantland: "In 2002 or '03,
this director got sick before he was gonna

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:46.170
direct me in something [...] When they went
to replace him, he suggested me, because I

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.500
had worked on the script with him.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.080
I said 'Really?

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.540
You recommended me?'

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.340
But I ended up directing the movie."

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:59.080
That movie was 2004's The Defender, and Lundgren
has gone on to write several more screenplays,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.760
and directed himself in five more features.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.960
He has multiple projects in the pipeline as
a director, but he's not content to stay behind

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:12.480
the camera completely; he's also been quietly
making a comeback in mainstream Hollywood

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:12.980
films.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.340
"Who is the most underrated actor of all time?"

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.340
"It's Dolph Lundgren."

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:16.840
"Correct.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:17.340
Why?"

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.780
"Well because of his spiky hair and his ice
cold demeanor and his big muscles."

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:21.280
"Absolutely."

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.300
Not so Expendable

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:31.200
In 2010, Lundgren's 15-year streak of non-theatrical
releases came to a close with The Expendables,

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:36.520
which brought together some of the greatest
action stars of the '80s for an unapologetically

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.180
throwback action film.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.820
In an interview with Den of Geek around the
time of the film's release, Lundgren said

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:47.239
he'd been wondering if he'd ever get to work
with Stallone again, as the two had remained

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.900
friends ever since they punched the crap out
of each other in Rocky IV.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:57.020
His Expendables character, the unhinged assassin
Gunnar Jensen, ended up being among his favorites

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.680
to play, and he reprised the role in the two
sequels.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.420
Television time

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:08.920
Since returning to Hollywood, Lundgren has
also taken on a number of one-shot and recurring

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:14.109
roles in various television series, beginning
with a small part in Chuck in 2010.

00:03:14.109 --> 00:03:19.060
In 2013, he was cast as a lead in the syndicated
TV series SAF3.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:24.260
While well-received, the Baywatch-style series
only lasted 20 episodes.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:30.420
More recently, Lundgren has appeared as himself
in episodes of Sanjay and Craig and Workaholics.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.000
"Why does Dolph Lundgren wanna do some dumb
health blood thing with us?"

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.680
"Oh, I don't know, maybe 'cause he's a decent
person who wants to contribute to society?"

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:44.920
He's also picked up a recurring role as Russian
government agent Konstantin Kovar on the CW's

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.260
hit superhero series Arrow.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:48.720
Fit Forever

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.220
Now past his 60th birthday, Lundgren still
isn't the kind of man you want to slap around.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.700
This is due to a lifetime of dedication to
fitness that began well before he became a

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:04.200
movie star.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:09.540
He won two karate titles in the early '80s
on his way to achieving a third-degree black

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:14.769
belt, thanks to an insane training regimen
that saw him rising at 3:00am every morning

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:19.960
for a five-mile run before hitting the dojo,
followed by hours of weight lifting, then

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.180
even more karate.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.740
Decades later, his training routine is only
slightly less intense.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:32.100
Heavy on sandbags, medicine balls, and dumbbells,
Lundgren prefers a resistance-based workout

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:37.550
routine, which keeps him in Drago-like shape
at an age when most men are starting to think

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:38.550
about retirement.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:43.640
He even outlined his complete approach to
fitness in his 2014 book Train Like an Action

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:50.040
Hero: Be Fit Forever, which details his "personal
philosophy of fitness based on martial arts,

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:56.120
yoga, strength training, biochemical research,
professional sports, and over 40 starring

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:00.640
roles in classic action films", which are
pretty decent qualifications.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:09.640
"Class dismissed."

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.800
Action activist

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:18.100
The 2014 film Skin Trade, which Lundgren wrote
and starred in, features him as an American

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.360
police officer who goes to Thailand to take
down a human trafficking ring.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.060
The film's supporting cast includes martial
arts legend Tony Jaa, iconic character actor

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.340
Ron Perlman, and Peter Weller, AKA Robocop.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:36.300
But its subject matter is no joke for Lundgren,
who's made it his mission to do everything

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.820
he can to call attention to the issue of human
trafficking.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:46.080
He works regularly with a Los Angeles non-profit
called Coalition to Abolish Slavery and Trafficking.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.640
Most action stars can be seen on the big screen
saving people from the clutches of criminal

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.340
gangs, but Lundgren may be the only one who's
taken up the pursuit in real life.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.120
Big return

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.800
Lundgren's perseverance, professionalism and
outright likability has paid off.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:09.580
Sure, he continues to appear in such future
non-classics as Sharknado 5: Global Swarming.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.480
"I'll save you, Nova!"

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.400
But his return to the big screen was received
warmly enough that offers haven't ended with

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:21.810
the Expendables series.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:28.300
He joined the cast of James Wan's DC Films
entry, Aquaman, as King Nereus, an antagonist

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:34.080
who covets the title character's love interest,
making a showdown with Jason Momoa's Aquaman

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.200
all but inevitable.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.860
But even more exciting is the news surrounding
the sequel to 2015's Creed.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.599
Stallone and Lundgren both dropped hints on
social media teasing the eventual confirmation

00:06:45.599 --> 00:06:50.660
that Lundgren would be returning to the role
that made him famous, which makes sense, given

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:55.910
that the storyline focuses on the fight between
Adonis Creed and Viktor Drago, Ivan Drago's

00:06:55.910 --> 00:07:00.519
son, tying the burgeoning spinoff franchise
back in with the saga that spawned it.

